  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer Sunday with widespread showers and possible\n thunderstorms. Sharp cold front crosses the area Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nShowers are on their way out this afternoon with clearing\nexpected tonight. Cold front will cross the area Sunday evening\nwith models depicting anafrontal structure with numerous to\nwidespread showers expected Sunday afternoon into Monday morning.\nA slight risk of thunderstorms is also possible, but the threat\nof severe wx remains low. Drying is expected late Monday morning\nas cdfnt pushes further to the south. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Late-season frost/freeze expected Monday night west \nof the Blue Ridge, followed by moderating temperatures and returning \nrain chances.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure builds overhead Mon night. Winds go light/calm \nresulting in favorable conditions for radiational cooling. This will \nresult in light winds and favorable radiational cooling conditions. \nFrost or near freezing temps could result in the sheltered valleys \nnear and west of I-81. This could be rather impactful given the fact \nthat we are approaching mid-May at this point which is a couple \nweeks to a month past the median last freeze for these areas.\n\nReturn flow returns Tue with high moving offshore. The warming trend \nwill be more noticeable heading into Wed as a warm front lifts into \nthe region, followed by a cold front and wave of low pressure Wed \nnight into Thu. This frontal system will bring a chance of showers \nand a few thunderstorms mid week, with guidance split on exactly how \nmuch rain falls over the area. Rain forecast to be beneficial in \nnature given ongoing drought.\n\nTemps will actually trend warmer in the wake of the frontal system \nat the end of next week as large scale height rises commence.",
